@startuml
title Three controls, and where an over-budget allocation actually stops

skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam roundCorner 10
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam defaultFontColor #111827
skinparam ArrowColor #4B5563
skinparam ArrowFontColor #374151
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #4B5563
  FontColor #111827
  BackgroundColor #F8FAFC
}

rectangle "Over-budget GPU allocation\non a 32 GB M1 Max" as start #EDE9FE

rectangle "mx.set_wired_limit\nResidency cap on non-pageable memory\nBounds the wired set; does not reject the allocation" as wired #FEF3C7
rectangle "mx.set_memory_limit\nAdvisory guideline\nDoes not stop evaluation; allocation may continue into swap" as soft #FEF3C7
rectangle "Excess buffers stay unwired and pageable\nAllocation continues toward RAM plus swap" as pageable #FEF3C7

rectangle "Before the fix: no process-local stop\nSustained pageable GPU pressure\nKernel panic at IOGPUMemory.cpp:550\n(driver trigger unverified)" as panic #FECACA
rectangle "After the fix: active-memory watchdog\nSamples mx.get_active_memory() about twice per second\nCrosses min(28.0 GiB static, available_at_start - 2 GiB)\nWrites aborted_memory_ceiling, then hard-exits" as abort #DCFCE7

start -down-> wired
wired -down-> soft : does not reject
soft -down-> pageable : does not reject
pageable -down-> panic
pageable -down-> abort

caption Layered view of the incident and its mitigation. The panic trigger is an unverified hypothesis; the watchdog is a best-effort process-local guard, not a hard machine boundary.
@enduml
